<section class="sj-section sj-hero" id="welcome">
  <div class="sj-wrap">
    <span class="sj-eyebrow">Orientation</span>
    <h1>Seeking Jesus of Nazareth</h1>
    <p class="sj-lede"><em>Common faith, real differences, and the Restoration—a map you can inspect.</em></p>
    <p>Christians who look to Jesus of Nazareth for salvation describe God in remarkably similar words and, in a few important places, in very different ones. This study lets you see where. It compares {{ C.counts.inherited }} ways the historic creeds and confessions describe God with what the Restoration teaches, one statement at a time, and it shows its sources for every claim.</p>
    <p>It is not a contest. Nothing here will tell you which church is true. It will help you say, with precision and charity, where two traditions genuinely agree, where they share a concern but mean it differently, where they directly disagree, and where one of them makes a claim the other does not.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="sj-section" id="commitments">
  <div class="sj-wrap">
    <h2>Five commitments</h2>
    <p>Before you begin, this study makes five promises, and it asks you to make them too.</p>
    <ol class="sj-commitments">
      <li><b>We will not mock other Christians.</b> Every tradition on this map is described in its own words, from its own official sources.</li>
      <li><b>We will not hide real differences.</b> Where two traditions disagree, the map says so plainly and shows the texts.</li>
      <li><b>We will not confuse similar words with identical meanings.</b> “Creator” is confessed by everyone here; what lies behind the word is not always the same.</li>
      <li><b>We will not confuse unity with uniformity.</b> Shared devotion to Christ does not require agreement on every doctrine.</li>
      <li><b>We will seek both truth and charity.</b> Accuracy without kindness wounds; kindness without accuracy deceives.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

